That's a **24-hour workload cert issued at 08:17 today**. And `rk5c5` earlier showed `cx_connect_fail::15` / `rq_error::15` against your pod while `gfvnc` showed zeroes. That's a per-connection handshake failure on one replica — consistent with an mTLS trust mismatch, not a routing problem.

## Do the definitive test: reproduce and read the exact failure

Everything static looks healthy, so capture the failure live. Trigger a request and immediately read what the gateway logs:

```bash
# terminal 1 — watch both gateway replicas
oc logs -n int-istio-system -l istio=int-istio-gateway -f --tail=5

# terminal 2 — reproduce
curl -sk https://integration.sbiepay.sbiuat.bank.in/api/integration/v1/actuator/health -v 2>&1 | tail -20
```

Read the Envoy response flag in the gateway log line — this is the single most important datum:
- `UF` / `UH` = upstream connection failure / no healthy upstream
- `UC` = upstream terminated connection (handshake reset)
- The `TLS_error` / `CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED` will reappear here if it's mTLS

## Test the gateway→pod path bypassing everything else

Confirm whether the gateway can even open a plain TCP connection to the pod (benign, no `/dev/tcp`):

```bash
# From the gateway pod, can it reach the pod IP:port at all?
oc exec -n int-istio-system int-istio-gateway-77d47dbb5-gfvnc -- \
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" --max-time 5 http://172.16.34.39:9099/api/integration/v1/actuator/health

# And check the gateway's live mTLS handshake stats to your cluster
oc exec -n int-istio-system int-istio-gateway-77d47dbb5-gfvnc -- \
  curl -s localhost:15000/stats | grep -iE "integration.*ssl|integration.*connect_fail|integration.*handshake"
```

If the plain-HTTP curl to `172.16.34.39:9099` returns a code (even 401/404), the pod is reachable and serving — narrowing it to the mTLS layer. If it times out, the problem is network/CNI reachability from gateway node to pod node.

## Check the pod actually came up healthy on the app side

The app container may be up (`2/2 Running`) but rejecting requests. Check its logs and readiness:

```bash
oc logs integration-integrationservice-577c88dcd-8blbm -c integrationservice -n int-integration --tail=50 \
  | grep -iE "error|started|exception|listen|9099|context-path"

# Confirm the app is actually serving on 9099 with the right context path
oc exec -n int-integration integration-integrationservice-577c88dcd-8blbm -c integrationservice -- \
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" --max-time 5 http://localhost:9099/api/integration/v1/actuator/health
```

Note from your ConfigMap: the app has `server.servlet.context-path: /api/integration/v1` **and** the VS routes `/api/integration/v1` without rewriting. So the app expects the full path `/api/integration/v1/...` — good, that matches. But confirm the app is actually listening; a Spring Boot app that failed Liquibase migration or Kafka/Oracle init can be "Running" but not serving on 9099.

Given your ConfigMap points at Oracle `epaydbscanuat.sbiepay.sbi:1524` and Kafka `int-kafka-cluster-kafka-bootstrap`, and `liquibase.enabled: true` with `drop-first: false` — if the DB or Kafka is unreachable, the app context may not have fully started, so 9099 accepts TCP (sidecar) but the app returns resets. That would produce `upstream connect error` at the gateway even though everything mesh-side is healthy.

